# Never commit credentials — Kalshi keys can trade, not just read.
*.pem
*.key
.env
secrets/
kalshi_*.pem

# Raw CSV download caches — 56MB and fully regenerable, never commit.
data/raw/csv/
data/raw/new_csv/
data/processed/*.parquet

# The raw parquets ARE committed, deliberately. Together they are ~9MB and
# they let a CI run start instantly instead of rebuilding them.
#
# Without this seed every cold GitHub Actions run had to execute
# refresh_all.py (~11.5 min) before it could predict anything, and the only
# thing that wrote the cache was the DAILY job — so until that first fired,
# every 15-minute run started cold, bootstrapped, and was killed by the job
# timeout. Seven consecutive runs cancelled in exactly that step.
#
# CI never commits these back: auto_update.publish_self_state writes an
# explicit path list (ledger, calibration, public/sports.json) and data/raw is
# not on it. So this is a one-time ~9MB seed, not per-run churn. Freshness
# still comes from the Actions cache and the daily refresh.

# Keep the ledger — it is the prediction record, not a cache
!data/processed/ledger.jsonl

# Keep cricket/T20I raw data. Unlike the other data/raw sources, nothing in
# this repo can regenerate these — there is no fetch_cricket.py equivalent
# wired into refresh_all.py (Cricsheet has no clean incremental endpoint).
# Everything else in data/raw is cheaply refetchable from a live API, so it
# rides GitHub Actions cache instead of git history; these files cannot be,
# so they must be committed or a cache eviction quietly breaks cricket.
!data/raw/cricket_hundred_*.parquet
!data/raw/t20i_male_*.parquet

# Auto-update's own lock/log files — local run state, not part of the record.
reports/auto_update.lock
reports/auto_update.log

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
